</ul><h1>Temperature Check</h1><p>If you watch enough cooking competition shows (and trust me, I have; in fact, I’ve probably watched too many), you learn there is one mistake that simply cannot be overcome.</p>
<p>And that mistake is bleeding in the food. Safety, ya know?</p>
<p>But after that, the second worst thing you can do is undercook your protein. Anything else can be overcome. Missing a required ingredient, an overdressed salad, sloppy presentation, lack of seasoning, too much seasoning, too much heat, too much sweet, using onions in a dessert–these things earn demerits but will prevail when put up against a protein that’s not properly cooked through. Even a protein that’s <em>overcooked</em> can get a pass.</p>
<p>Which makes sense. After all, an undercooked protein ranges from unpleasant on the good end all the way down to absolutely unsafe to eat.</p>
<p>But <em>you</em>? You won’t have to worry about that. Not with the MeatStick 4 on your side. Or, more accurately, stabbed <em>into</em> the side of whatever cut you’re cooking.</p>
<p>Basically what we have here is a WiFi-enabled temperature probe. You put it into the cut of meat, put the cut of meat into the oven, and you let it cook. The probe will then send updates and “CookTime Alerts” to your phone or even your Apple Watch. Does this sound a little silly? Sure. And usually we love to roll our eyes at “Smart” tech that does the same thing as stupid tech. But in this case, it’s actually practical. Since you can see the temp on your phone, you don’t have to open the oven to check if your roast is done, letting out hot air and slowing things down in the process.</p>
<p>It also, we should note, works without WiFi if you’re, say, camping. You just put the Bridge in Xtender mode and can get readings from up to 300 feet away.</p>
<p>Why, considering how convenient it is, it’s no wonder it’s endorsed by some of the biggest, meatiest movie stars there are. You know, luminaries such as:</p>
